            "Respondents to web surveys systematically score higher on knowledge questions than respondents to interviewer-administered modes, and aural versus visual modes experience different rates of selecting the first or last (or more positive) response options, so different modes of data collection yield different measurement biases.",
            "Surveys increasingly use multiple modes of data collection, called mixed-mode surveys, which can be beneficial for participation and coverage when sample members have different mode preferences or access to different technology, and modes may be offered simultaneously (concurrent) or in successive contact attempts (sequential, sometimes called web-push when one mode is web).",
            "Mixed-mode designs take advantage of the strengths of different modes, including computerization for navigation and reduced data entry costs, while mitigating weaknesses of individual modes such as lack of access to technology.",
